            </section>
            <section xml:id="quizzes_and_exams">
            <title>Quizzes and Exams</title>
                <p>  Four exams, worth 35-45 points each will be administered throughout the semester, along with a cumulative final exam.  Each student's lowest exam score will be replaced by an average of the student's quiz and homework scores.  Five to six quizzes, worth 15 points each, will be administered throughout the semester, with the lowest quiz score being dropped from the student's course point total.</p>
                <p> All assignments will be largely based on the textbook examples and exercises and the accompanying online homework assignments.  Approximately 20% to 40% of all in-class assignments will be mastery questions (multiple choice, true/false, matching, and fill-in-the-blank style), which will generally not be graded based upon student work.  In general, all open-response questions will not receive partial credit unless sufficient work has been shown.</p>
            </section>
            <section xml:id="homework_and_recitation">
            <title>Homework and Recitation</title>
                <p> This course includes a required online homework component, worth 40 course points.
                    <!-- Student homework scores contributing to their final grade calculation will be based upon a maximum of 90% of all assigned homework problems.-->  
                    More information regarding these this requirement will be discussed during class.</p>
                <!--<p> This course includes a required recitation hour.  Student attendance and completion of all recitation assignments will count for 30 points towards the course grade.</p>-->
                <p> It is also recommended that students complete as many problems from the textbook and course handouts as time permits for a more complete understanding of key concepts.  Assigned written homework sets will only be collected in class for grading purposes when the instructor deems it necessary.  Students are strongly encouraged to discuss homework problems with their classmates, as well as the instructor and designated supplemental instructor, either during office hours or at the start of class.</p>
            </section>
            <section xml:id="attendance_and_participation">
                    <title>Attendance and Participation</title>
                        <p>Attendance will be taken during every 50-minutes of in-person or remote instruction, and counts for 25 course points.  Students who have been observed by the instructor as either frequently absent or late to class or non-participatory may be subject to point deductions from their overall course score.  Students are responsible for obtaining lecture notes from their classmates for any classes missed.  At any point during the semester, students may be asked to provide proper documentation to the Office of Student Affairs, justifying any absence from the University.</p>
                        <p> Successful completion of this course requires active participation on the part of the student.  To help promote classroom engagement and motivate student success, the instructor will make every effort to encourage active participation in a supportive learning environment.  Consequently, students will be expected to attend any lecture or recitations on time and ready to contribute.  Any student who cannot meet these expectations for whatever reason should notify the instructor formally with their concerns.</p>
            </section>
</chapter>
